hypotheses.statement:
Let $\Pi_{\mathrm{pair}}$ be the quotient of $\Pi$ obtained by killing the boundary loops of its 24 two-puncture disks. It is free of rank 24, and its pro-2 completion is naturally $$\label{eq:pinched-sphere-group}
 \widehat{\Pi_{\mathrm{pair}}}
 \simeq L/\overline{\langle\!\langle
              \sigma(g)g^{-1}:g\in L
                    \rangle\!\rangle}.$$ More precisely, let $\lambda:L\twoheadrightarrow R$ be a continuous quotient of pro-2 groups satisfying $\lambda\sigma=\lambda$. There is a homomorphism $\theta:\Pi\to R$ with dense image and $$\label{eq:pinching-square}
 \theta p=\lambda\Phi|_K.$$ For $m\in\Gamma$, the condition $$\label{eq:torus-equalizer-condition}
 \lambda\Phi(\varphi_m(k))=\lambda\Phi(k)
 \qquad(k\in K)$$ implies $\theta\beta_m=\theta$ on the entire unpinched group $\Pi$.

Let $L$ be the free pro-$2$ group on $a_{h,j}$, with $h\in(\mathbb Z/4)^2$ and $0\le j\le2$, and let $\sigma(a_{h,j})=a_{b_j-h,j}^{-1}$, where $b_j\bmod2\ne0$. Equip $L$ with the basis and weights of [lem:shift-basis](#lem:shift-basis). There are 24 elements $g\in L$, with assigned positive weights $w_g$, such that $$\nu_L(g)\ge w_g,\qquad
 \nu_L(r_g)\ge w_g+1,
 \qquad r_g=\sigma(g)g^{-1},$$ and the closed normal subgroup generated by the $r_g$ imposes $\sigma=\mathop{\mathrm{id}}$ on all of $L$. The basis polynomial and the polynomial of the chosen weights are $$\label{eq:weight-polynomials}
 \begin{split}
 P(t)&=\sum_{i,\ell,j}t^{i+\ell+1}
       =3t(1+t+t^2+t^3)^2,\\
 G(t)&=\sum_g t^{w_g}
       =3t(1+t)(1+t^2)^2.
 \end{split}$$ In particular, $P=(1+t)G$, and these involution relations have degree cost at most $tG(t)$.

